<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Virbhadra Singh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1934">1934</a>–<a href="/years/2021">2021</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Virbhadra Singh was the last Raja of the princely state of Bashahr in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalaya</a> and an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> politician. After the state merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> he served six terms as Chief Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himachal-pradesh">Himachal Pradesh</a> between 1983 and 2017, as well as a member of parliament and union minister.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/virbhadra-singh.jpg" alt="Virbhadra Singh" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Snjsharma · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Virbhadra_Singh_HP.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Virbhadra Singh was born on 23 June 1934 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarahan">Sarahan</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimla">Shimla</a> district, into the royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> family of Bushahr. He was the 122nd titular Raja of Bushahr from 1947 until 1971, when the 26th Amendment to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-india">Constitution of India</a> abolished the privy purses of the princes and ended official recognition of their titles. He was still known by the honorific Raja Sahib.</p><p>He was educated at Colonel Brown Cambridge School in Dehradun and at St. Edward's School and Bishop Cotton School in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimla">Shimla</a>, and took a BA Honours degree at St. Stephen's College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. In 1954 he married Ratna Kumari, princess of Jubbal, who died in 1983; in 1985 he married Pratibha Singh, princess of Keonthal, who was herself later elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lok-sabha">Lok Sabha</a> from Mandi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Singh first entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lok-sabha">Lok Sabha</a> in 1962 and was returned in 1967, 1971, 1980 and 2009. He served in the governments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indira-gandhi">Indira Gandhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manmohan-singh">Manmohan Singh</a>: as Deputy Minister for Tourism and Civil Aviation in 1976-1977, Minister of State for Industries from 1980 to 1983, Minister for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">Steel</a> from May 2009 to January 2011, and then minister for Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises until June 2012.</p><p>In state politics he entered the Himachal Pradesh Legislative Assembly at a by-election in October 1983 for Jubbal-Kotkhai, later sitting for Rohru and from 2012 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimla">Shimla</a> rural. He was first Chief Minister from April 1983 to March 1990, again from December 1993 to March 1998, and from March 2003; between 1998 and 2003 he was Leader of the Opposition. He led the Congress to victory in November 2012 and resigned after the party lost its majority in 2017.</p><p>His career was shadowed by corruption cases. A case registered in 2009 by the state Anti-Corruption Bureau led to his resignation from the Union Cabinet on 26 June 2012; he and his wife were acquitted that December. In 2015 the Central Bureau of Investigation filed a case alleging assets disproportionate to his income while a union minister.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Singh suffered a cardiac arrest on 5 July 2021 at Indira Gandhi Medical College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimla">Shimla</a>, after testing positive for COVID-19 a second time in June, and died of multiple organ failure on 8 July 2021, still sitting as the member for Arki. The state observed three days of mourning, and he was cremated with full state honours at Rampur Bushahr on 10 July. Over 21 years in office he remained the longest-serving Chief Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himachal-pradesh">Himachal Pradesh</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Virbhadra Singh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its last Raja, <b>Virbhadra Singh</b>, later served six terms as Chief Minister of Himachal Pradesh.</blockquote><a href="/state/bashahr-state">Bashahr State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarahan">Sarahan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimla">Shimla</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.52,77.8&amp;year=1990" aria-label="Sarahan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6557 1016 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Virbhadra Singh"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7007 1286h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7007 1286h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.52,77.8&amp;year=1990">What was here in 1990? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
